## Exactly-Once with Transactions

```java
@Configuration
public class KafkaTransactionConfig {

    @Bean
    public KafkaTransactionManager<String, Object> kafkaTransactionManager(
            ProducerFactory<String, Object> producerFactory) {
        return new KafkaTransactionManager<>(producerFactory);
    }
}

@Service
public class PaymentProcessor {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, Object> kafkaTemplate;

    // Atomically: process payment + publish event + commit Kafka consumer offset
    @Transactional  // uses KafkaTransactionManager
    public void processPayment(PaymentEvent payment) {
        // These all happen atomically in one Kafka transaction:
        PaymentResult result = chargeCard(payment);

        kafkaTemplate.send("payment-results", payment.getOrderId(), result);
        kafkaTemplate.send("order-updates",  payment.getOrderId(),
            new OrderStatusEvent(payment.getOrderId(), "PAID"));

        // If any send fails or an exception is thrown:
        // → transaction rolls back → consumer offset not committed → messages reprocessed
    }
}
```

## Consumer Idempotency (Practical Approach)

```java
// Often simpler than full EOS transactions:
// Make the consumer operation idempotent — safe to process twice
@Service
public class OrderProcessor {

    public void process(OrderEvent event) {
        // Check if already processed using the event ID
        if (processedEventRepository.existsByEventId(event.getEventId())) {
            log.info("Skipping duplicate event: {}", event.getEventId());
            return;
        }

        // Process...
        orderRepository.updateStatus(event.getOrderId(), OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);

        // Mark as processed
        processedEventRepository.save(new ProcessedEvent(event.getEventId()));
    }
}
```

## Custom Partitioner

```java
public class PriorityPartitioner implements Partitioner {

    @Override
    public int partition(String topic, Object key, byte[] keyBytes,
                         Object value, byte[] valueBytes, Cluster cluster) {
        int numPartitions = cluster.partitionCountForTopic(topic);

        // First 2 partitions reserved for VIP messages
        // Remaining for regular messages
        int vipPartitions = Math.max(2, numPartitions / 4);
        int regularPartitions = numPartitions - vipPartitions;

        if (value instanceof OrderEvent event && event.isVip()) {
            // Hash into VIP partition range [0, vipPartitions)
            return Math.abs(key.hashCode()) % vipPartitions;
        } else {
            // Hash into regular partition range [vipPartitions, numPartitions)
            return vipPartitions + (Math.abs(key.hashCode()) % regularPartitions);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void configure(Map<String, ?> configs) {}

    @Override
    public void close() {}
}
```

## Register Custom Partitioner

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    producer:
      properties:
        partitioner.class: com.myapp.kafka.PriorityPartitioner
```

## Hot Partition Problem

```
Problem: key="user:popular" generates 10,000 msg/s → partition 3 overwhelmed
         other partitions: ~100 msg/s each

Solution A: Compound key — "user:popular:suffix" where suffix = random(0,5)
  → distributes hot key across 5 partitions
  → trade-off: loses strict ordering for this user

Solution B: Custom partitioner detects hot key, distributes with suffix
  → same trade-off but automatic

Solution C: Separate topic for hot producers with more partitions
  → preserves ordering on their dedicated topic
```

